{
  "abstract": "A ten-chromatic biplanar graph contains a 10-critical biplanar graph $G$ on at least twenty vertices. The order-twenty programme on the r/earth_moon forum fixes an independent triple $S$ and splits on the order $t$ of a 9-critical subgraph $X$ of $G-S$. Earlier papers settle $t\\in\\{11,\\dots,16\\}$; we settle the last branch, $t=17$, where $X$ spans $G-S$ and therefore is a core: a $K_9$-free 9-critical graph on 17 vertices with at most 81 edges. We first prove that there are exactly 534 cores up to isomorphism. Of these, 225 have independence number 2 (26 clique blow-ups of $C_5$ and 199 others) and 309 have independence number 3. The first part is a geng enumeration of triangle-free complements with exact criticality filters; the second is a new $K_8$-anchored lazy-Hall SAT enumeration over all 9-vertex graphs, pruned by the Gallai–Kostochka–Stiebitz–Wirth theorem on low vertices. It shows that the 309-class forum inventory is complete and recovers the 22- and 223-class subfamilies of earlier papers. We then prove that every admissible 20-vertex extension of a core is 9-colourable. For 13 blow-up types we assume $\\alpha(G-s)\\ge3$ for each $s\\in S$, which holds in every 10-critical graph. One exact CEGAR encoding over all 9-colourings of the core is used, and every final clause set is refuted with an LRAT or DRAT proof checked by lrat-check or drat-trim. For the $C_5$ blow-ups we add a symmetry reduction by twin-class count vectors with lexicographic symmetry breaking, and we prove it sound. Corollary: if $G$ is a 10-critical biplanar graph on 20 vertices, then for no independent triple $S$ does $G-S$ have a spanning 9-critical subgraph. Combined with the published branches $t=11$, $t\\in\\{12,\\dots,15\\}$ and $t=16$, this shows that every biplanar graph on at most twenty vertices is 9-colourable. The supplement contains the cores, code, logs, all certificate records and a checker.",
